---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import CycleFocus from '../../components/CycleFocus.astro'
---
<Base
  title="Cycle and Pregnancy Calculators"
  description="Period, ovulation, cycle-length and due-date calculators, plus a pregnancy weeks-to-months converter. Every one runs locally and transmits nothing."
  path="/tools"
>
  <h1 class="display text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.6rem]">Calculators</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose">
    These tools work from information you already have — a last period, an
    average cycle length, a handful of recent start dates, a gestational age
    from an appointment — using the same arithmetic built into the LunarFlow
    app. Each one runs as plain JavaScript in your own browser: nothing you
    type into a calculator on this page is sent anywhere, and using one
    doesn't require the app itself.
  </p>

  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted dark:text-white/60">
    The track under each cycle calculator marks the part of a 28-day cycle it
    can speak to.
  </p>

  <h2 class="display-sm mt-12 text-[1.6rem]">From your cycle</h2>

  <div class="mt-7 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-9 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/period-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-menstrual">Period calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <CycleFocus from={1} to={5} color="menstrual" label="Bleeding days" class="mt-4" />
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Estimate your next three period start and end dates from your last
        period and your average cycle and period length.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/ovulation-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-ovulatory">Ovulation calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <CycleFocus from={10} to={16} color="fertile" label="Fertile window" class="mt-4" />
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Estimate a likely ovulation day and fertile window by counting
        backward from your next expected period. Not a method of
        contraception.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/cycle-length-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-follicular">Cycle length calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <CycleFocus from={1} to={28} color="follicular" label="The whole cycle, end to end" class="mt-4" />
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Enter a few recent period start dates to see your average cycle
        length, its spread, and whether that pattern looks regular.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/due-date-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-predicted">Due date calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <CycleFocus from={1} to={1} color="predicted" label="Counts forward from day 1 only" class="mt-4" />
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Estimate a due date and trimester timeline from your last period and
        cycle length, using Naegele's rule adjusted for cycle length.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 class="display-sm mt-14 text-[1.6rem]">Between ovulation and a test result</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted dark:text-white/60">
    These start from a cycle like the ones above, but the dates they return run
    past the end of it, so there is no 28-day track that would show them honestly.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-7 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-9 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/pregnancy-test-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-fertile">Pregnancy test calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        The earliest date a home test is worth taking, and the date after which a
        negative is no longer explained by having tested too early.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/implantation-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-ovulatory">Implantation calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        The days implantation would most likely fall on, from your cycle and — if
        you have measured it — your own luteal phase length.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <h2 class="display-sm mt-14 text-[1.6rem]">From a pregnancy figure</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-sm text-ink-muted dark:text-white/60">
    These start from a pregnancy figure or date rather than from a cycle, so
    there is no 28-day track to mark.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-7 grid gap-x-10 gap-y-9 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/pregnancy-weeks-to-months" class="pressable hover:text-follicular">Pregnancy weeks to months</a>
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Convert a gestational age in weeks and days into a pregnancy month and
        trimester, with the exact day each boundary falls on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/ivf-due-date-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-predicted">IVF and FET due date calculator</a>
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Estimated due date from an embryo transfer date and the embryo age at
        transfer, with both reference dates the 280-day convention is built on.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-outline pt-5 dark:border-white/15">
      <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.35rem]">
        <a href="/tools/ultrasound-due-date-calculator" class="pressable hover:text-predicted">Due date by ultrasound</a>
      </h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-sm">
        Estimated due date from a dating scan, and how it compares with a
        last-period estimate against the published redating thresholds.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-prose">
    <h2 class="display-sm text-[1.4rem]">What these estimators can and cannot tell you</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Every calculator here does the same kind of thing: it takes a small
      number of dates you already know and projects forward or backward
      using averages drawn from general reproductive-health references, not
      from anything specific to your own body beyond the numbers you typed
      in. That makes them useful for a quick, rough estimate — planning
      around a likely period, getting a sense of a fertile window, working
      out roughly how far along a pregnancy is — without needing to open an
      app or dig up a paper calendar.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      It also sets a hard limit on what they can tell you. Each calculator
      assumes a single fixed cycle length repeating indefinitely, and real
      cycles vary — sometimes by a few days, sometimes by a lot more, for
      reasons ranging from illness and travel to hormonal contraception and
      perimenopause. None of these tools can detect irregularity, diagnose a
      condition, or tell you why a cycle changed; they can only do the
      arithmetic on the assumption you fed them. They are not a method of
      contraception, and a result from any of them is not a substitute for
      advice from a doctor or midwife — see the disclaimer on each
      calculator's page for the specifics.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      The gap between a calculator and something like the{' '}
      <a href="/features" class="underline">predictions built into LunarFlow</a>{' '}
      is history. A calculator only ever sees the numbers you give it in one
      sitting; ongoing tracking sees your actual cycles, one after another,
      and can adjust as your real pattern shifts instead of staying anchored
      to a single guess. If a calculator's estimate keeps missing, that's
      usually a sign your cycle has more going on than one average can
      capture — worth logging properly, and if it's a sudden or sustained
      change, worth mentioning to a doctor too.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
